Data-flow patterns that turned out to be right in more than one place. Proven
useful, therefore worth reaching for first the next time something looks similar.
Several of these arrived from sibling projects in this monorepo with their
evidence already attached.

--

## Generate, then view. Never both at once.

The dune builder produces a heightfield; something else draws it. The simulation
produces snapshots; something else renders them. The headless runner produces a
report; something else reads it. The Markdown is the data; the HTML is a view of
it. The tileset generator produces images; the viewer only ever loads them.

Every time this split exists, a bug has a side, and the search space halves.
Every time it does not, a wrong pixel and a wrong rule look identical.

## A periodic effect is a pair of integers.

One counter per effect, ticking for everyone at once. A unit records the counter's
value when it subscribed. To know how many times the effect has fired for that
unit: subtract. To know whether it fires this tick: compare the counter's
increment to the tick. Four operations, no per-unit timer, no per-unit state
beyond one integer.

This is the vision's own pattern and it generalises past healing: reload,
shield recharge, engineer recovery, the cloud's fight rounds, the discovery
broadcast on the radio. Reach for it before reaching for a per-unit countdown.

## Copy at the boundary. Never hold a reference across it.

A tank carries its own copy of the pattern it follows, the damage it deals, and
the falloff curve it deals it on. Nothing in the firing path dereferences a
factory, a team record, or a catalogue to find out how strong something is.
Copy at the boundary between something that changes rarely and something that is
read constantly, and let the fast side touch only itself.

The design consequence -- a unit keeps what it was born with -- is the vision's
"they don't listen after they've left the factory", arrived at from the other
direction. Fast and right often agree. Look for that.

## Flat arrays, integer zero for "nothing", a validator at load.

Every record is a row in a struct-of-arrays. A field that has no value holds the
integer zero, not an absent value. Whether the builder got that right is a
validator's question, asked once at load, not the tick's question asked a
thousand times.

## Intent goes through one door, and the door is a queue.

Every player action is a command with a tick stamp, appended to one queue, applied
at that tick. "Everything can be queued" is the vision's invariant; the network
gets its whole design from it, because a queue that already tolerates a command
arriving early tolerates one arriving from another machine.

## Errors over fallbacks.

A fallback is a warning and a warning is an error. If a lookup fails, stop and say
what was looked for and where. A default silently substituted turns one bug into a
mystery discovered three systems downstream. The tests in this project are built
on this: a missing module is a named absence, not a skipped test.
